 ### Smoke Color Mapping
-Smoke Color Mapping determines the diffuse color of the smoke. It requires external light to become visible if GI is enabled. The `difSource` parameter specifies the source channel (Temperature, Smoke, Speed, Texture, Constant Color, RGB, Fuel). If `difSource` is not Texture, the color is multiplied by the texture in the `difTexture` slot if `difModulate` is enabled. The `difScattering` parameter controls light scattering (Ray-traced, Disabled, Approximate) [S1].
+Smoke Color Mapping determines the diffuse color of the smoke volume. It requires external light to become visible if GI is enabled [S1]. The `Based on` (`difSource`) parameter specifies the source channel: Constant Color (default), Temperature, Smoke, Speed, Texture, RGB, or Fuel [S1]. If `Based on` is not Texture, the color can be modulated by a texture map (`difModulate`) [S1]. The `Scattering` (`difScattering`) parameter controls light ray scattering, with options for Ray-traced (GI only, slowest, most realistic), Disabled (uses Diffuse Multiplier for brightness), and Approximate [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of steps per ray, which is determined by `rendstep` and the grid resolution. Shadow rays add additional complexity. Grid-based motion blur requires a pre-pass, increasing time [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the voxel grid size and the number of channels stored. Grid-based motion blur requires additional memory for pre-pass data [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Box sampler is the fastest. Linear is 20-30% faster than Spherical. Spherical is the smoothest but slowest [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with grid resolution and the number of lights. Increasing `rendstep` or `rendshadstep` improves performance but reduces quality [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides detailed control over volumetric shading for Phoenix FD and V-Ray Volume Grids. Supports various displacement types and motion blur methods [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** `Render as Geometry` slows down rendering. Grid-based motion blur requires more memory. Standard Maya materials cannot be used [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When rendering Fire/Smoke simulations from Phoenix FD or V-Ray Volume Grids in Maya.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When using standard Maya materials or when performance is critical and high quality is not required (use Box sampler) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of ray steps, which is inversely proportional to `Step %` and `Shadow Step %`. Shadow rays add additional cost, especially with many lights [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the size of the voxel grid and any additional textures (Velocity, Displacement) [S1]. Grid-based motion blur requires a pre-pass and more memory [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Box sampler is fastest. Linear is 20-30% faster than Spherical. Spherical is smoothest but slowest [S1]. "Render as Geometry" slows down rendering significantly [S1]. Grid-based motion blur is slower than Ray-traced due to pre-pass [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance degrades with higher resolution grids and lower step percentages [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Supports complex volumetric shading, motion blur, displacement, and render elements (via Render as Geometry) [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** "Render as Geometry" is slow. Grid-based motion blur requires more memory. Step % < 90% increases render time [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** For Fire/Smoke simulations where volumetric shading is required and standard materials cannot be used [S1].
